; em4100_tag.loom
; An EM4100 read-only 125 kHz RFID tag at logic level: the tag counts the
; cycles of the reader's field on RF and sends its 64-bit frame on MOD,
; Manchester coded at RF/64 (or RF/32), repeated for as long as it runs.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). The tag has no clock of its
; own: every bit time is a count of input-pin edges, not a deadline, so the
; program never uses the deadline timebase at all (no SETD, no WAITD). It
; uses:
;   - the Manchester encoder of the bit engine (SEMANTICS 6.9.1, ENC = 2),
;     built for 10 Mbit/s IEEE 802.3 Manchester, the target D-029 cut (it
;     stayed for the L3-MANCH loopback): a bit is two SHO, the engine keeps
;     the half-bit phase. The EM4100's code is the same convention (below);
;   - WAITP on an input pin and DJNZ as an edge counter, the idea of the
;     quadrature programs (quad_decoder.loom polls levels with JP): here the
;     level waits WAITP RF, 0 / WAITP RF, 1 count field cycles, so a field
;     edge that comes while the thread is busy is not lost, as it would be
;     for WAITE (SEMANTICS 6.4: PREV_PINS is refreshed by every slot);
;   - CNT and Z of the engine as the bit counter of a 16-bit word, so that
;     the next word is loaded in the second half of a word's last bit,
;     after the bit has left SR (6.9.1: a data bit leaves SR on the first
;     SHO of its two).
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: the frame itself (header,
; the ten even row parities, the four column parities, the stop bit) built
; from the 40-bit ID, the division of the field by 32 or 16 per half-bit,
; and the endless repetition. Weaker than the real chip: the tag needs a
; host to give it its ID (an EM4100 is laser programmed), and its MOD edge
; lags the field edge by 12 to 15 clocks, where the chip's own logic is
; clocked by the field directly (the data sheet gives no lag at all).
;
; Source: EM Microelectronic-Marin, EM4100 data sheet (copyright 2004):
; "Memory Array for Manchester & Bi-Phase encoding ICs" (p. 5): 9 header
; bits of 1, then 10 rows of 4 data bits D?0..D?3 and an even row parity
; P0..P9, then 4 even column parity bits PC0..PC3 and a stop bit S0 = 0,
; "outputted serially" and "repeated continuously until power goes off";
; "Code Description, Manchester" and Fig. 6 (p. 5): a transition in the
; middle of every bit; on "Modulator control" a 1 is low then high and a 0
; high then low, and "low means high current" (the load switch on);
; "Sequencer" (p. 4) and "Ordering Information" (p. 8): 64 or 32 field
; periods per bit (versions A6 and A5). Bits go out in the table's order,
; row by row, D?0 first. The data sheet does not say which field edge the
; sequencer counts or where a bit starts against the field; this tag
; changes MOD after the rising edge of RF that completes a half-bit's count.
;
; Pins: RF = IN1, the reader's field as a logic square wave (what COIL1's
; clock extractor would give); MOD = OUT1, push-pull, carrying Fig. 6's
; "Modulator control": 1 = load switch off, 0 = load switch on. A reader
; whose envelope detector reads high when the field is unloaded sees MOD
; itself on its DEMOD input; the bench wires MOD to DEMOD. MOD is 1 (no
; load) from the start until the first half-bit.
;
; Host words, INQ[2], three of them, read once after RUN:
;     word 0   bits 7:0 ID[39:32] (D00..D13: the version or customer
;              byte), bit 8: 1 = RF/32, 0 = RF/64; other bits ignored
;     word 1   ID[31:16]           word 2   ID[15:0]
; D00 is ID[39], D93 is ID[0]: the ID is the ten nibbles in the order they
; are sent. To change the ID the host resets the thread and pushes again.
; Nothing is reported on OUTQ[2].
;
; Frame build. The 64 bits are appended one at a time to the chain
; r0:r1:r2:r3 (app), so r0 ends up holding bits 0..15 (the header and D00
; D01 D02 D03 P0 D10 D11), r3 bits 48..63. Each row's parity comes from the
; four bits just appended (r3 & 15) with PAR; the column parities are the
; XOR of the ten nibbles. Building takes about 4,000 clocks (measured:
; under 4,200), during which MOD stays 1.
;
; Transmit. r5 = N field cycles per half-bit (32 or 16). Each half-bit is
; N passes of WAITP RF, 0; WAITP RF, 1; DJNZ, then SHO, which drives the
; half's level on MOD (ENC = 2, DIR = MSB first). After the first-half SHO
; of a word's last bit CNT is 0 and Z set: that half's count is split in
; three chunks of one field cycle each, in which the next word goes into SR
; and the four words rotate (r0 is always the word being sent, r1 the next)
; and CNT is reloaded with 16, so the frame runs on without a gap.
;
; Field edge to MOD edge (SEMANTICS 2 and 3). A rising edge of RF sampled
; first at edge s completes WAITP RF, 1 in an X cycle x in s+1..s+4; DJNZ
; is at x+4 and SHO at x+8, and the pad changes at edge x+10. So every MOD
; edge comes 12 to 15 clocks after the RF rise that ends a half-bit's
; count (the bench drives a pad for a whole cycle and the chip samples it
; at the edge that ends the cycle). The longest path from a completed
; WAITP RF, 1 to the next WAITP RF, 0 is five slots (x+20, after a SHO, a
; MOV and a BZ or JMP), and that WAITP must still find the low half of the
; same field cycle: the field period must be at least 24 clocks with a 50 %
; duty cycle, and each half at least 4 (one slot). 125 kHz is 400 clocks at
; 50 MHz; the RTL tests run the field at 32 clocks per period to stay in
; the simulation budget, and the model tests sweep it down to the limit.
;
; Registers: r0..r3 the frame words; r4 the field-cycle count; r5 N;
; r6 the column parity while building, then 16; r7 the bit to append, then
; scratch. Memory (.imem 512): thread 2's quarter, from 0x100, so the tag
; runs beside em4100_reader.loom (threads 0 and 1).
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07):
;   Which field edge is counted and where a bit starts: the data sheet does
;   not say; the choice is in the model. The reader finds the bit phase
;   from pulse lengths, so it does not depend on it.
;   Bit order in a row (D00 first, then the row parity): from memory,
;   unchecked against the data sheet's figure.
;   MOD and DEMOD are logic level as the brief defines them; a front end
;   that inverts needs only BE_CFG.INV.
;   The 8 version bits are treated as ID bits, with the same parity rules.
;   The RTL runs use a fast field (32 and 40 clocks a period); 125 kHz
;   runs on the model.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .pins   RF = IN1, MOD = OUT1
        .equ    CFG = (2 << 3) | (1 << 1)       ; ENC Manchester, DIR MSB first

        .thread 2
        SETP    MOD, 1                  ; no load until the first half-bit
        .csr    BE_PINS, pin(MOD)       ; out = MOD
        LDI     r6, 0                   ; the column parities
        LDI     r7, 1
        LDI     r5, 9
head:   CALL    app                     ; the nine header bits, all 1
        DJNZ    r5, head
        POP     r4                      ; word 0: rate bit 8, ID[39:32]
        LDSR    r4                      ; kept in SR until the rate is needed
        SHLI    r4, 8                   ; D00 to bit 15
        LDI     r5, 2
        CALL    rows                    ; rows 0 and 1
        POP     r4                      ; ID[31:16]
        LDI     r5, 4
        CALL    rows                    ; rows 2 to 5
        POP     r4                      ; ID[15:0]
        LDI     r5, 4
        CALL    rows                    ; rows 6 to 9
        MOV     r4, r6
        SHLI    r4, 12                  ; PC0 to bit 15
        CALL    bit                     ; PC0 .. PC3
        CALL    bit
        CALL    bit
        CALL    bit
        LDI     r7, 0
        CALL    app                     ; S0, the stop bit: 64 bits appended

        STSR    r7                      ; word 0 again
        SHRI    r7, 8
        ANDI    r7, 1                   ; Z = (RF/64)
        LDI     r5, 32
        BZ      rate
        LDI     r5, 16
rate:   LDI     r6, 16                  ; bits per word
        .csr    BE_CFG, CFG             ; also clears HALF (6.9.1)
        LDSR    r0                      ; word 0 of the frame
        CSRW    CNT, r6
        MOV     r4, r5

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- transmit
c1:     WAITP   RF, 0                   ; one field cycle: a low ...
        WAITP   RF, 1                   ; ... and the rising edge after it
        DJNZ    r4, c1
        SHO                             ; first half of a bit: CNT - 1
        MOV     r4, r5
        BZ      last                    ; CNT = 0: the word's last bit
c2:     WAITP   RF, 0
        WAITP   RF, 1
        DJNZ    r4, c2
        SHO                             ; second half (Z ignored)
        MOV     r4, r5
        JMP     c1

; the second half of a word's last bit: SR is free; one field cycle a chunk
last:   WAITP   RF, 0
        WAITP   RF, 1
        LDSR    r1                      ; the next word
        MOV     r7, r0
        MOV     r0, r1
        WAITP   RF, 0
        WAITP   RF, 1
        MOV     r1, r2
        MOV     r2, r3
        MOV     r3, r7                  ; rotated: r0 is the word in SR
        WAITP   RF, 0
        WAITP   RF, 1
        CSRW    CNT, r6                 ; so the second-half SHO leaves Z clear
        SUBI    r4, 3                   ; three of the N cycles are spent
        JMP     c2

; ------------------------------------------------------------- frame build
; rows: r5 rows from r4 (left-aligned nibbles), each four bits and parity
rows:   CALL    bit
        CALL    bit
        CALL    bit
        CALL    bit
        MOV     r7, r3
        ANDI    r7, 15                  ; the nibble just appended
        XOR     r6, r6, r7              ; into the column parities
        PAR     r7, r7                  ; C = odd number of 1s
        LDI     r7, 0
        BNC     rpar
        LDI     r7, 1                   ; even parity: the row's P bit
rpar:   CALL    app
        DJNZ    r5, rows
        RET

; bit: append the top bit of r4 and shift r4 left
bit:    LDI     r7, 0
        SHLI    r4, 1                   ; C = the bit
        BNC     app
        LDI     r7, 1
; app: append r7 (0 or 1) to the 64-bit chain r0:r1:r2:r3
app:    SHLI    r0, 1
        SHLI    r1, 1                   ; C = r1's top bit, into r0
        BNC     a1
        ORI     r0, 1
a1:     SHLI    r2, 1
        BNC     a2
        ORI     r1, 1
a2:     SHLI    r3, 1
        BNC     a3
        ORI     r2, 1
a3:     OR      r3, r3, r7
        RET
